\begin{letter}{Editor-in-Chief\\
Artificial Intelligence in Medicine}

\opening{Dear Editor-in-Chief,}

\textbf{Subject: Submission of Original Manuscript: An interpretable block-term tensor network with a separate clinical term for predicting incident atrial fibrillation from one sinus-rhythm 12-lead ECG}

We submit the manuscript named above for consideration as a Research Article in Artificial Intelligence in Medicine.

\textbf{What the paper addresses.} Electrocardiogram models that predict incident atrial fibrillation are explained only after training, by attribution methods that agree poorly with one another. The paper asks whether a model whose ECG evidence passes through readable frequency and lead factors, with clinical risk factors in a separate linear term, predicts incident atrial fibrillation more accurately than a clinical model and a convolutional neural network.

\textbf{Key findings.} On coded diagnoses in the MIMIC-IV hospital records linked to MIMIC-IV-ECG, the proposed network with its clinical term had the highest one-year area under the receiver operating characteristic curve of the methods compared: 0.7776 against 0.7597 for the clinical model, with overlapping 95\% confidence intervals. Its ECG term alone was comparable to a convolutional neural network (0.7397 against 0.7350), and per unit of signal energy it responded most to the PR window of the beat.

\textbf{Why this journal.} The paper develops an interpretable machine-learning model for a clinical prediction task and evaluates it against clinical and deep-learning baselines with calibration, risk stratification and operating points.

We confirm the following:
